1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,297
In the summer of 1944, families across

2
00:00:03,317 --> 00:00:06,190
Germany got the news that every sailor's

3
00:00:06,210 --> 00:00:07,017
family feared.

4
00:00:07,037 --> 00:00:09,910
The submarine U five-oh-five was long overdue.

5
00:00:09,930 --> 00:00:12,803
Her crew, the German Navy told them,

6
00:00:12,823 --> 00:00:14,890
had to be considered dead.

7
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:17,690
They were not dead.

8
00:00:17,710 --> 00:00:19,577
Fifty-eight of them were alive,

9
00:00:19,597 --> 00:00:22,596
behind American barbed wire in a corner of

10
00:00:22,616 --> 00:00:24,483
a prison camp in Louisiana.

11
00:00:24,503 --> 00:00:27,124
No letter of theirs left the camp.

12
00:00:27,144 --> 00:00:30,143
The Red Cross was not told their names.

13
00:00:30,163 --> 00:00:33,560
Their own country had no idea where they were.

14
00:00:34,775 --> 00:00:37,247
The rules of war said their families

15
00:00:37,267 --> 00:00:38,315
should have known.

16
00:00:38,335 --> 00:00:41,163
The United States Navy decided they could not.

17
00:00:41,183 --> 00:00:43,299
One American captain had already written

18
00:00:43,319 --> 00:00:45,079
down why the secret mattered,

19
00:00:45,099 --> 00:00:49,015
in a memo to his men, under the words top secret.

20
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:52,746
This is the documented story of the crew

21
00:00:52,766 --> 00:00:54,904
of U five-oh-five, told from the Navy's

22
00:00:54,924 --> 00:00:56,446
own reports, from their captain's

23
00:00:56,466 --> 00:00:58,603
statement, and from what came out after

24
00:00:58,623 --> 00:00:59,423
the war.

25
00:01:00,950 --> 00:01:03,637
U five-oh-five was a long-range submarine,

26
00:01:03,657 --> 00:01:07,170
commissioned in August 1941.

27
00:01:07,190 --> 00:01:08,999
She did not stay lucky.

28
00:01:09,019 --> 00:01:12,905
In November 1942, a bomb from an Allied

29
00:01:12,925 --> 00:01:15,522
patrol plane nearly sank her in the Caribbean.

30
00:01:15,542 --> 00:01:17,290
She limped home to Lorient,

31
00:01:17,310 --> 00:01:18,110
in France.

32
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:22,500
After months of repairs she put to sea

33
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:24,840
again, and turned back within days.

34
00:01:24,860 --> 00:01:26,790
Then she did it again.

35
00:01:26,810 --> 00:01:30,300
Six times in a row she came home early,

36
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:32,250
with one defect after another.

37
00:01:32,270 --> 00:01:34,980
Sabotage by French dockyard workers was suspected.

38
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,120
Other U-boat crews made a joke of her.

39
00:01:39,325 --> 00:01:42,625
In October 1943, near the Azores,

40
00:01:42,645 --> 00:01:45,025
two Allied destroyers caught her with

41
00:01:45,045 --> 00:01:45,825
depth charges.

42
00:01:45,845 --> 00:01:47,545
Her captain, Peter Zschech,

43
00:01:47,745 --> 00:01:49,607
shot himself in the control room,

44
00:01:49,627 --> 00:01:51,176
in front of his crew.

45
00:01:51,196 --> 00:01:53,705
His second in command brought the boat home.

46
00:01:54,975 --> 00:01:58,890
In November 1943 she got a new captain,

47
00:01:58,910 --> 00:02:00,806
Harald Lange, a reserve officer.

48
00:02:00,826 --> 00:02:04,602
By late spring 1944 she was off the coast

49
00:02:04,622 --> 00:02:06,995
of West Africa, at the end of a long patrol.

50
00:02:08,825 --> 00:02:12,178
The Americans hunting her had an unusual plan.

51
00:02:12,198 --> 00:02:14,286
Captain Daniel Gallery commanded Task

52
00:02:14,306 --> 00:02:15,972
Group twenty-two point three:

53
00:02:15,992 --> 00:02:18,501
the escort carrier Guadalcanal and five

54
00:02:18,521 --> 00:02:19,344
destroyer escorts.

55
00:02:19,364 --> 00:02:22,717
He did not want to sink a U-boat.

56
00:02:22,737 --> 00:02:24,845
He wanted to take one.

57
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:28,089
Before the cruise, Gallery ordered every

58
00:02:28,109 --> 00:02:31,102
ship to plan for a capture and to organize

59
00:02:31,122 --> 00:02:31,771
boarding parties.

60
00:02:31,791 --> 00:02:34,784
The task group's plan of the day for the

61
00:02:34,804 --> 00:02:37,463
fourth of June was printed the evening before.

62
00:02:37,483 --> 00:02:40,476
It already listed the men who would crew a

63
00:02:40,496 --> 00:02:41,500
captured German submarine.

64
00:02:43,375 --> 00:02:45,440
At ten past eleven in the morning on the

65
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:48,944
fourth of June, 1944, two days before

66
00:02:48,964 --> 00:02:50,875
D-Day, the destroyer escort Chatelain

67
00:02:50,895 --> 00:02:52,936
reported a possible sound contact,

68
00:02:52,956 --> 00:02:56,233
about a hundred and fifty miles off the

69
00:02:56,253 --> 00:02:57,881
coast of West Africa.

70
00:02:57,901 --> 00:02:59,942
Six minutes later she fired.

71
00:02:59,962 --> 00:03:02,827
Two fighter pilots overhead could see the

72
00:03:02,847 --> 00:03:04,495
submarine below the surface.

73
00:03:05,825 --> 00:03:08,485
Inside the boat, Lange had seen three

74
00:03:08,505 --> 00:03:10,782
destroyers through his periscope and dived.

75
00:03:10,802 --> 00:03:12,696
Then came two heavy shocks.

76
00:03:12,716 --> 00:03:13,845
Water came in.

77
00:03:13,865 --> 00:03:16,908
The lights went out and the rudders jammed.

78
00:03:16,928 --> 00:03:19,205
Not knowing how bad it was,

79
00:03:19,225 --> 00:03:21,905
he ordered the boat to the surface.

80
00:03:23,250 --> 00:03:25,050
She came up eight hundred yards from

81
00:03:25,070 --> 00:03:27,190
Chatelain, and the escorts and fighters

82
00:03:27,210 --> 00:03:27,870
opened fire.

83
00:03:27,890 --> 00:03:29,910
Lange was hit in both legs,

84
00:03:29,930 --> 00:03:32,970
and splinters cut his face and his right eye.

85
00:03:32,990 --> 00:03:36,030
A blast threw him down onto the main deck.

86
00:03:36,050 --> 00:03:39,090
He ordered his men to leave the boat and

87
00:03:39,110 --> 00:03:40,130
to sink her.

88
00:03:41,325 --> 00:03:43,203
They opened valves and jumped.

89
00:03:43,223 --> 00:03:45,480
But the engines were still running,

90
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:47,757
and the empty submarine kept circling.

91
00:03:47,777 --> 00:03:51,173
From a life raft, Lange had the men around

92
00:03:51,193 --> 00:03:54,210
him give three cheers for their sinking boat.

93
00:03:54,230 --> 00:03:56,487
At eleven twenty-seven, the Navy recorded

94
00:03:56,507 --> 00:03:58,405
the official time of surrender.

95
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:03,145
A boat from the destroyer escort Pillsbury

96
00:04:03,165 --> 00:04:03,993
came alongside.

97
00:04:04,013 --> 00:04:06,958
Lieutenant Albert David led eight men onto

98
00:04:06,978 --> 00:04:08,229
the circling submarine.

99
00:04:08,249 --> 00:04:11,618
One German sailor lay dead on the deck.

100
00:04:11,638 --> 00:04:14,180
He was the only man killed.

101
00:04:15,575 --> 00:04:17,876
David went down the hatch first,

102
00:04:17,896 --> 00:04:21,359
not knowing if the boat was rigged to explode.

103
00:04:21,379 --> 00:04:24,454
His men closed a valve that was letting

104
00:04:24,474 --> 00:04:27,549
the sea in, and started passing up papers:

105
00:04:27,569 --> 00:04:30,258
codebooks, charts, and an Enigma cipher machine.

106
00:04:30,278 --> 00:04:32,966
A second boarding party found and disarmed

107
00:04:32,986 --> 00:04:34,127
thirteen demolition charges.

108
00:04:34,147 --> 00:04:36,855
The fourteenth was only found weeks later.

109
00:04:38,125 --> 00:04:39,673
The carrier's chief engineer,

110
00:04:39,693 --> 00:04:41,704
Earl Trosino, kept her afloat.

111
00:04:41,724 --> 00:04:44,175
He let the turning propellers drive the

112
00:04:44,195 --> 00:04:45,587
electric motors as generators,

113
00:04:45,607 --> 00:04:46,999
to recharge the batteries,

114
00:04:47,019 --> 00:04:49,824
so her own pumps could empty her tanks.

115
00:04:49,844 --> 00:04:51,942
The Guadalcanal took her in tow,

116
00:04:51,962 --> 00:04:54,766
and on the nineteenth of June she was

117
00:04:54,786 --> 00:04:55,845
towed into Bermuda.

118
00:04:57,275 --> 00:04:59,838
If you want more prisoner of war stories

119
00:04:59,858 --> 00:05:02,555
told from the documents, subscribe to WWII

120
00:05:02,575 --> 00:05:03,024
Frontlines.

121
00:05:03,044 --> 00:05:05,855
It helps us keep finding them.

122
00:05:07,475 --> 00:05:09,703
Now the Navy had a problem.

123
00:05:09,723 --> 00:05:12,326
The codebooks were only useful if Germany

124
00:05:12,346 --> 00:05:15,698
believed the boat had gone down with all hands.

125
00:05:15,718 --> 00:05:17,946
About three thousand American sailors had

126
00:05:17,966 --> 00:05:18,766
seen it.

127
00:05:19,900 --> 00:05:21,424
On the fourteenth of June,

128
00:05:21,444 --> 00:05:24,204
Gallery wrote to every man in the task group.

129
00:05:24,224 --> 00:05:27,475
The capture of the U-505 can be one of the

130
00:05:27,495 --> 00:05:29,623
major turning points in World War number

131
00:05:29,643 --> 00:05:31,772
two provided repeat provided we keep our

132
00:05:31,792 --> 00:05:33,020
mouths shut about it.

133
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:37,029
The enemy must not learn of this capture,

134
00:05:37,049 --> 00:05:37,741
he wrote.

135
00:05:37,761 --> 00:05:40,591
Their friends would read about it one day

136
00:05:40,611 --> 00:05:42,015
in the history books.

137
00:05:42,035 --> 00:05:44,864
In Bermuda the crews were told to stay

138
00:05:44,884 --> 00:05:47,357
silent, and every souvenir was turned in.

139
00:05:47,377 --> 00:05:49,494
According to the historian Clay Blair,

140
00:05:49,514 --> 00:05:52,343
Admiral Ernest King was so angry at the

141
00:05:52,363 --> 00:05:54,500
risk that he considered court-martialling Gallery.

142
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:58,468
The bigger risk was the prisoners.

143
00:05:58,488 --> 00:06:00,656
Fifty-eight Germans could tell their navy

144
00:06:00,676 --> 00:06:02,844
that the boat had not sunk.

145
00:06:02,864 --> 00:06:05,761
One letter home, or one Red Cross visit,

146
00:06:05,781 --> 00:06:07,240
could end the secret.

147
00:06:08,550 --> 00:06:11,450
In the summer of 1944 they were taken to

148
00:06:11,470 --> 00:06:13,251
Camp Ruston, in northern Louisiana.

149
00:06:13,271 --> 00:06:16,555
It was a big camp, about seven miles west

150
00:06:16,575 --> 00:06:19,957
of the town, built in 1942 on seven

151
00:06:19,977 --> 00:06:21,450
hundred and seventy acres.

152
00:06:21,470 --> 00:06:24,437
At its peak it held more than four

153
00:06:24,457 --> 00:06:27,050
thousand prisoners, the first of them from

154
00:06:27,070 --> 00:06:28,190
Rommel's Afrika Korps.

155
00:06:29,375 --> 00:06:32,333
The U five-oh-five men did not join them.

156
00:06:32,353 --> 00:06:35,684
They were kept in an isolated part of the

157
00:06:35,704 --> 00:06:37,918
camp, away from every other prisoner.

158
00:06:37,938 --> 00:06:40,897
Their names were withheld from the Red Cross.

159
00:06:40,917 --> 00:06:43,895
The letters they tried to send were confiscated.

160
00:06:45,375 --> 00:06:47,767
The rules of war said prisoners could

161
00:06:47,787 --> 00:06:50,524
write home, and that the Red Cross would

162
00:06:50,544 --> 00:06:51,902
know where they were.

163
00:06:51,922 --> 00:06:54,658
The National World War Two Museum put the

164
00:06:54,678 --> 00:06:55,692
Navy's view plainly.

165
00:06:55,712 --> 00:06:57,435
It could take no chances.

166
00:06:58,825 --> 00:07:00,625
Around them, other prisoners picked

167
00:07:00,645 --> 00:07:02,809
cotton, cut timber and built roads,

168
00:07:02,829 --> 00:07:04,629
for pay in camp money.

169
00:07:04,649 --> 00:07:07,905
They had an orchestra, a theater and a library.

170
00:07:07,925 --> 00:07:10,817
How much of that life the isolated sailors

171
00:07:10,837 --> 00:07:13,385
shared, the published record does not say.

172
00:07:15,050 --> 00:07:18,110
In Germany, the boat was long overdue.

173
00:07:18,130 --> 00:07:21,923
By August 1944, the German Navy had told

174
00:07:21,943 --> 00:07:24,281
the families of her crew that their men

175
00:07:24,301 --> 00:07:25,459
must be considered dead.

176
00:07:25,479 --> 00:07:27,817
For the rest of the war, their families

177
00:07:27,837 --> 00:07:29,310
believed them lost at sea.

178
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:32,740
On the eighth of May, 1945,

179
00:07:32,760 --> 00:07:33,514
Germany surrendered.

180
00:07:33,534 --> 00:07:36,224
Eight days later, the Navy finally told

181
00:07:36,244 --> 00:07:36,999
the world.

182
00:07:37,019 --> 00:07:39,709
It called the capture the first boarding

183
00:07:39,729 --> 00:07:42,806
of a foreign enemy warship on the high

184
00:07:42,826 --> 00:07:45,167
seas since 1815.

185
00:07:45,187 --> 00:07:46,978
Fifty-eight of the fifty-nine Germans

186
00:07:46,998 --> 00:07:49,513
aboard had survived, and were prisoners in

187
00:07:49,533 --> 00:07:50,620
the United States.

188
00:07:51,900 --> 00:07:54,086
Albert David received the Medal of Honor,

189
00:07:54,106 --> 00:07:57,238
the only one given to a Navy man in the

190
00:07:57,258 --> 00:07:58,499
Battle of the Atlantic.

191
00:07:58,519 --> 00:08:01,020
He died of a heart attack in September

192
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:04,112
1945, before he could receive it.

193
00:08:04,132 --> 00:08:06,920
President Truman gave it to his widow.

194
00:08:08,250 --> 00:08:11,270
Only after the war did the families learn

195
00:08:11,290 --> 00:08:12,030
the truth.

196
00:08:12,050 --> 00:08:14,830
The last prisoners left Camp Ruston in

197
00:08:14,850 --> 00:08:17,350
February 1946.

198
00:08:17,370 --> 00:08:19,843
The crew of U five-oh-five did not get

199
00:08:19,863 --> 00:08:22,900
home to Germany until 1947.

200
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,335
The Navy kept the submarine.

201
00:08:26,355 --> 00:08:29,161
Gallery's brother, a priest in Chicago,

202
00:08:29,181 --> 00:08:32,458
contacted the city's Museum of Science and

203
00:08:32,478 --> 00:08:32,929
Industry.

204
00:08:32,949 --> 00:08:36,650
In 1954 the Navy gave the boat to the

205
00:08:36,670 --> 00:08:38,599
museum, and people in Chicago raised two

206
00:08:38,619 --> 00:08:40,270
hundred and fifty thousand dollars to

207
00:08:40,290 --> 00:08:42,240
bring her in through the Great Lakes.

208
00:08:43,575 --> 00:08:47,095
In 1964, twenty years after the capture,

209
00:08:47,115 --> 00:08:50,695
Gallery and Lange met at a reunion at the museum.

210
00:08:50,715 --> 00:08:53,215
Gallery gave the German captain back his

211
00:08:53,235 --> 00:08:54,035
binoculars.

212
00:08:54,750 --> 00:08:57,390
For years, the museum told its visitors

213
00:08:57,410 --> 00:09:00,050
the crew had been held in Bermuda.

214
00:09:00,070 --> 00:09:03,105
In 1998, a researcher brought it

215
00:09:03,125 --> 00:09:04,519
declassified National Archives records

216
00:09:04,539 --> 00:09:06,642
showing they had been in Louisiana,

217
00:09:06,662 --> 00:09:08,430
and the exhibit was corrected.

218
00:09:09,775 --> 00:09:12,295
If your family has a story about the Navy

219
00:09:12,315 --> 00:09:14,271
in the Atlantic, or about a German

220
00:09:14,291 --> 00:09:15,964
prisoner held in the American South,

221
00:09:15,984 --> 00:09:17,395
tell us in the comments.

222
00:09:19,150 --> 00:09:21,868
In 2004, the museum moved U five-oh-five

223
00:09:21,888 --> 00:09:24,214
into a new hall below ground,

224
00:09:24,234 --> 00:09:27,343
and she reopened on the fifth of June,

225
00:09:27,363 --> 00:09:30,863
2005, sixty-one years and a day after her capture.

226
00:09:30,883 --> 00:09:33,670
In Louisiana, two buildings of Camp Ruston

227
00:09:33,690 --> 00:09:35,654
are still standing, on the National

228
00:09:35,674 --> 00:09:36,977
Register of Historic Places.

229
00:09:36,997 --> 00:09:39,954
They are what is left of the camp where

230
00:09:39,974 --> 00:09:42,269
fifty-eight men listed as dead waited out

231
00:09:42,289 --> 00:09:42,950
the war.

